WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.080
Let's talk about jobs. We are starting to see&nbsp;
better and better jobs numbers as coronavirus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:12.080
cases have been declining, hospitalizations have&nbsp;
been declining. Deaths, while still tragically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.760
high, are also declining. And hopefully&nbsp;
this is going to continue. In February,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.720
nonfarm payrolls went up three hundred and seventy&nbsp;
nine thousand, even though the expectation was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.960
only two hundred and ten thousand. So the real&nbsp;
numbers exceeding the expectations by about 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.360
percent more. In fact, the unemployment rate was&nbsp;
expected to be six point three percent. It came in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:44.160
lower at six point two percent. And this is, of&nbsp;
course, just the start. There's a huge way to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.960
to recover from this pandemic. The pandemic&nbsp;
is nowhere near over 2000 people a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.880
are still dying every day in the United States,&nbsp;
although we're starting to point to below those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.160
numbers. But this is still something. Now, let's&nbsp;
do the normal disclaimers on the show. If you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.720
a new viewer or a new listener, I don't play&nbsp;
these Republican Democrat games with jobs numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.520
and with the unemployment rate. I know that&nbsp;
a lot of shows they do this with gas prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.680
They do this with debt and deficit. They do this&nbsp;
with unemployment numbers and jobs numbers. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.080
is not the show if you're looking for that&nbsp;
garbage. First of all, this has very little to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:29.040
do with Joe Biden so far. Joe Biden is rolling out&nbsp;
vaccines much faster than Donald Trump was. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:35.200
good. And that is starting to help cases go down&nbsp;
that has the effect of helping businesses to open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.960
but that moves really, really slowly. This is&nbsp;
not really about Joe Biden right now. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.960
result of broader economic circumstances, the&nbsp;
pandemic. And it has little to do with Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.680
nor Trump, although for sure, had we had a real&nbsp;
adult response to the pandemic from the federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.800
government starting a year ago when it was Trump,&nbsp;
we never would have lost as many jobs as we lost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.120
And of course, we never would have lost as many&nbsp;
people as we tragically lost. But we've talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.200
about this many times before. And when we talk&nbsp;
about jobs numbers and administrations, second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:15.200
lots of people play this game of, oh, David,&nbsp;
you're talking about the U3 when it's convenient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:21.280
but you should really be talking about the you&nbsp;
six. I hate to even have to do this again, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:27.120
as a reminder, there are many unemployment rates&nbsp;
when you hear on the news the unemployment rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:33.120
they are almost always talking about what's called&nbsp;
the U3. There is another unemployment rate called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.800
the you six. The you six includes the people that&nbsp;
we talk about when we say six point two percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:46.160
are unemployed. But you add to that discouraged&nbsp;
workers. In other words, those who have not found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.360
a job want one but are discouraged. They're not&nbsp;
even looking anymore. And you add in people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.720
are working part time but would rather be working&nbsp;
full time. They're only part time because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.160
can't find a full time job. And people love to&nbsp;
play games when the U3 goes down. But you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.520
criticize the president, you say, well, let's look&nbsp;
at the you six, which is always higher right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.040
Over the last month, the U.S. six is unchanged.&nbsp;
It's at eleven point one. There's no reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.560
hide that. Again, this is not really a story&nbsp;
about Joe Biden anyway. This is a story about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.200
the reality of the pandemic. I know lots of shows&nbsp;
love to mess with this stuff. We don't do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.880
The hospitality industry is still three and a&nbsp;
half million jobs short of where it was a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.280
ago before the pandemic exploded. And if you&nbsp;
look at total employment, we have a long way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.600
to go. There were more than one hundred and&nbsp;
fifty two million jobs before the pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.440
There are now one hundred and forty three million.&nbsp;
We are nine million jobs short of the pre pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.880
numbers, despite having more people living in&nbsp;
the United States than we did a year ago. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:52.720
another attack on these numbers that's meant to&nbsp;
hurt Joe Biden is people saying, well, but David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:59.520
a lot of these new jobs are part time jobs. That's&nbsp;
true. And there are some unique circumstances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:06.000
right now that explain that lots of businesses are&nbsp;
slowly reopening businesses that have been closed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.640
or places that have been reduced, for example, to&nbsp;
a restaurant that's takeout only that is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.360
start going in the direction of opening back up.&nbsp;
A lot of these businesses are bringing people back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:24.320
in for just a few days per week. And hopefully, if&nbsp;
all goes well, if we continue to do the things we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.920
have to do, many of those part time jobs will turn&nbsp;
to full time jobs as things continue to recover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:36.000
This is 90 percent about the reality of the&nbsp;
situation, not about Trump or Biden per say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.640
with the very important caveat that if Trump&nbsp;
had managed this well, it never would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:48.240
gotten as bad as it got. So the take away is slow&nbsp;
improvements. The better vaccination rollout goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.600
and the more people resist the urge to just&nbsp;
open everything wide open and go crazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.160
the more quickly these numbers are going to be&nbsp;
able to recover. If things go perfectly, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.600
listen, there's different estimates. If things go&nbsp;
perfectly, you could see the bulk of the recovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:12.000
happen during this calendar year. Some industries&nbsp;
are, even if everything goes as well as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.400
will not be fully recovered this year regardless.&nbsp;
And travel. Some estimates say travel won't fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.560
recover until summer of twenty, twenty two or&nbsp;
even later. I think, you know, things change so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:30.240
quickly that any predictions going so far out are&nbsp;
of limited value. But the point is, we still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.760
a long road ahead of us and it's made longer by&nbsp;
people like the ones I'm going to talk about next.

